content:
An if statement runs code only when a condition is true. Use indentation to show which lines belong to it.

if score >= 10:
    print("Great!")
else:
    print("Keep going")

A for loop visits each item in a collection. The indented body runs once for every item.

for color in ["red", "blue"]:
    print(color)

A dictionary stores key-value pairs. Use a key to look up its value.

person = {"name": "Lee", "age": 24}
print(person["name"])

Tuples are ordered and cannot be changed after creation. Sets contain unique values.

point = (3, 4)
tags = {"python", "learn"}

try and except let a program respond to an error instead of stopping unexpectedly.

try:
    number = int(input("Number: "))
except ValueError:
    print("Please enter digits")

The __init__ method sets up a new object. self refers to that particular object.

class Pet:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

Inheritance lets a new class reuse or extend another class.

class Dog(Animal):
    pass
